  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 146 PM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nEarly afternoon analysis showed upper level ridging amplifying \nacross the central CONUS with surface low pressure developing \nover northeastern WY and a surface high parked over the eastern \nCONUS. This left us under a tightening pressure gradient, \nbringing breezy south winds to the area, with gusts of 20-30 mph\nacross the area. The southerly flow was helping to bring warmer\nair to the area, with temperatures as of 1 PM sitting in the \nmid 50s for much of the area, though some pesky clouds in \nnortheast NE were keeping those areas a few degrees cooler.\n\nThe pressure gradient will further tighten on Tuesday and \nWednesday leading to even stronger winds, and in turn, even \nwarmer temperatures. EPS guidance shows mean wind gusts in the \n30-40 mph range with model soundings showing 40-50 mph winds at \nthe top of the mixed layer. Current forecast would keep us just \nunder Wind Advisory criteria, though we'll want to keep an eye \non trends, especially as more short term guidance comes in. \nRegardless, it'll be gusty and help temperatures climb into the \nlower to mid 60s on Wednesday.\n\nMeanwhile, the surface low in WY will push northeast on \nWednesday, eventually dragging a cold front into the area by \nlate afternoon/evening. Guidance continues to show less and less\nmoisture availability ahead of the front, so precip chances \ncontinue to be on the downward trend, and are generally 10% or \nless across the area.\n\nAttention then turns to additional precip chances Friday and \nSaturday as another trough and surface low push through the \nregion. Starting to be better agreement in track of the surface \nlow from northeast CO Friday morning, northeast through western \nNE during the day, and into the Dakotas by Saturday morning, \nthough still some small differences. With this farther north \ntrend, snow chances for the forecast area have nearly fully \ndiminished. In addition, with the mid-week system bringing in \nless moisture, there will be less moisture for the late week \nsystem to work with. In turn, precip chances have come down a \nbit, though still think we'll see some. First, expect some \nspotty precip on Friday as warm air and moisture advection ramp \nup (20-30% chance). Higher chances will arrive Friday night into\nSaturday as the surface low drags a cold front through the \nforecast area (40-70% chance, highest in northeast NE and west- \ncentral IA). Still can't rule out a few rumbles of thunder as \nthe front passes through, though with the diminished moisture \nreturn, instability is pretty minimal. Also still some \ndifferences in timing of the front, but a vast majority of \nguidance pushes precip east of the area by mid-day on Saturday, \nwith only a 20-30% chance after noon.\n\nOtherwise, temperatures will remain warm ahead of the system, \nwith mid to even upper 60s on Friday before the cold front \ncrashes through and brings us back to the reality that it is \nstill November. High temperatures on Saturday could be right at \nmidnight with daytime temperatures staying steady or falling \n(mid 40s to mid 50s through the day). Highs on Sunday and Monday\nthen look to be back down in the 40s.",
